From: nemo-speech-python
Subject: ggml: grow the scheduler's split buffers as splits need them

ggml_backend_sched_new() sized two things for the worst case of graph_size
up front: the context for the tensor copies and views made while splitting
a graph (graph_size*GGML_SCHED_MAX_SPLIT_INPUTS*2 tensors) and the four
backend id arrays (graph_size*61 entries each). NeMo-Speech.cpp creates
every scheduler with a graph_size of 65536, so each Session allocated about
1.3 GB, almost none of it used: its largest graphs have a few thousand
nodes and few or no copies.

Allocate both as splits need them. The backend id arrays grow together
with the graph copy, which already grows to the size the split computes
before writing them; new entries start zeroed as with calloc. The context
memory comes in chunks of doubling size, allocated when a split needs more
and kept until the scheduler is freed; every split reuses them in the same
order, so tensors of the previous split stay addressable as they did in the
single buffer. The hash set is unchanged: it holds user backend assignments
and its size keeps lookups fast.
---
